# THE STANDING GUARD FOR THE FCOV LEG'S FAILING DIRECTION: its first failing run, recorded.
# ASCII-NORMALISED per the retention convention: every character above 127 in a quoted artefact line, the em
# dash ci/check_fcov_expectations.py prints included, is written as one '-'; everything else is verbatim.
#
# WHAT THE GUARD IS. A committed testlist entry whose fixture manifest declares one bin the entry cannot hit, so
# every regression that runs it exercises the flow's refusal of an unmet declared bin. Before it, nothing committed
# made the flow FAIL when a declared bin went unhit: one retained control had exercised that once from a scratch
# root, which is a different claim from a permanent check every regression runs.
#   entry     gen_ut_lockstep_icache_ecc_fcov_red, tier check, red_fixture true, measured false
#   manifest  dv/auto_dv/fcov_expectations/gen_ut_lockstep_icache_ecc_fcov_red.fcov.yaml
#   bin       gen_ic_ecc_cg.cp_ram.data
#   grading   red_fixture plus red_expect. expected_fail is FALSE and must stay false: the loader refuses
#             red_fixture beside expected_fail, because a fixture is not an RTL-bug candidate.
#   red_expect  fcov expectation unmet: [0-9]+ declared bin\(s\) not hit .*gen_ic_ecc_cg\.cp_ram\.data
#
# WHY THE BIN CANNOT BE HIT, and it is by construction rather than by luck. cp_ram samples the injecting RAM of an
# announcement, tag as 0 and data as 1, and this entry's plusargs set the tag-RAM rate alone with no data-RAM knob,
# so no announcement of the run can carry data and no seed can turn the guard green. A HIT would be a real defect
# either way round: the tag hook announcing a data injection, or a data injection running with its knob absent. The
# same bin is declared and MET by gen_ut_lockstep_icache_ecc_data at 904 hits in that entry's own run, so the bin is
# reachable in general and only these plusargs cannot reach it.
#
# WHY THIS LOG LIVES HERE, under gen_tdd_logs/flow, and never under gen_tdd_logs/lockstep with the family's
# gen_{group}_red1_stdout naming: at that path the loader does not warn, it REFUSES the entry, and every flow command
# that reads the testlist then dies with a message that reads like a stale red signature rather than a path problem.
# The reason is structural: the failure's evidence is the expectation checker's own reason line, not a line in the
# simulation's stdout, so a replay looking for it in the stdout cannot find it. The group the naming would derive
# from is lockstep_icache_ecc_fcov. Measured by the Runtime Manager with a real failing stdout placed at that path.
#
# THE BUILD, AND ITS CAVEAT STATED UP FRONT RATHER THAN IN A FOOTNOTE. The run reused the build
# dv/auto_dv/work/runtime/out/l25_exercise_1430/build/gen_tb, identity b48479a3bc6f1d9f, which is the flow's
# filelist_digest over gen_rtl.f plus gen_tb.f and NOT the local runner's build_sources_sha256; its sources come
# from the mirror head tree of 813994b3d48340719bc4d5119e4f01ac177a4eda. THOSE SOURCES PREDATE THIS LANDING: the
# flow's own build-input comparison between that commit and HEAD reports 30 changed paths and the decision
# refused_build_inputs_changed, of which only two are non-inputs (gen_component_api_irq_checker.md and
# gen_runtime_api.md).
# WHY THE UNHITTABILITY ARGUMENT SURVIVES THAT, checked rather than assumed: gen_fcov_groups.svh is cd4c1f69fdfc and
# gen_fcov_pkg.sv is d4b339e24ff3 at BOTH that build's commit and HEAD, byte-identical, so cp_ram's bins are exactly
# the tag={0} and data={1} pair the note cites and the coverage database this run produced was built from the same
# covergroup HEAD carries. The 30 moved paths are the drain and knob work, none of which defines or samples cp_ram.
# THE LIMIT, PLAINLY: the entry and the manifest were UNCOMMITTED at run time and came from a scratch source root,
# so this run is evidence about the entry AS STAGED, not as committed. The canonical head-mode run of the committed
# entry follows as a supplement in the next touch. A build of the landing's own sources is not reachable before the
# entry is committed, because the build refuses a bound source root that carries no head-mode mirror manifest.

== 1. THE FAILING RUN, standalone with the expectation check ==
   Seed 1, program gen_icache_ecc_directed.S, unmeasured single seed.
$ command grep -a "GEN_RUN_VERDICT" g1/run.log
   GEN_RUN_VERDICT RED-OK reason=red fixture failed as designed (red_expect matched): fcov expectation unmet: 1 declared bin(s) not hit ['gen_ic_ecc_cg.cp_ram.data'] wall_s=8.4 exit_code=0

$ cat g1/fcov_check.log   (the last two lines, the checker's own)
   FCOV-EXPECTATION: gen_ic_ecc_cg.cp_ram.data = UNHIT (count=0)
   FCOV-EXPECTATION: FAIL - 1 declared bin(s) not hit: gen_ic_ecc_cg.cp_ram.data

$ python3 -c "read g1/result.yaml and print the fields the verdict rests on"
   verdict           = RED-OK
   evidence_line     = fcov expectation unmet: 1 declared bin(s) not hit ['gen_ic_ecc_cg.cp_ram.data']
   run exit_code     = 0  (the simulation itself passed)
   fcov_check exit   = 2  (the expectation check failed; this is the whole mechanism)
   fcov status       = UNHIT  declared = 1  hit = 0
   unmet_bins        = ['gen_ic_ecc_cg.cp_ram.data']
   manifest sha256   = 5afebcf87fa66d1e (first 16)
   manifest owner    = tb-infra

   READING: the simulation PASSED at exit 0 and the expectation check exited 2, and the red_fixture grading
   turned that into RED-OK. So the failure this guard produces is the checking mechanism alone, with no
   contribution from the stimulus, which is what makes it a guard on the mechanism rather than on the DUT.

   The manifest the run consumed is byte-identical to the one this landing commits:
$ cmp g1/fcov_manifest_used.yaml dv/auto_dv/fcov_expectations/gen_ut_lockstep_icache_ecc_fcov_red.fcov.yaml
   identical (cmp silent, rc 0); md5 fac44d438611e2f97047a55eae66cd4b

== 2. THE REGRESSION SHAPE, which is how the guard will actually run ==
   In a regression gen_run is invoked WITHOUT the expectation check, so it cannot judge the entry and parks it;
   the pre-merge pass then runs the check and grades it, rewriting result.yaml. The run log and the result file
   therefore DISAGREE by design, and a reader who sees only the run log would read the guard as not run:
$ command grep -a "GEN_RUN_VERDICT" g2/run.log
   GEN_RUN_VERDICT NOT_RUN reason=red fixture judged on an fcov expectation that no stage of this run checked (no coverage vdb, or the check was not requested) wall_s=7.5 exit_code=0
$ python3 -c "read g2/result.yaml after the pre-merge pass"
   verdict   = RED-OK
   reason    = red fixture failed as designed (red_expect matched): fcov expectation unmet: 1 declared bin(s) not hit ['gen_ic_ecc_cg.cp_ram.data']
   exit_code = 0
   READING: NOT_RUN in the run log, RED-OK in the rewritten result. The parked reason names exactly what was
   missing, a red fixture judged on an expectation no stage of that run checked, which is the deliberate third
   state introduced with the grading move: not a pass and not a failure, and counted as neither.

== 3. WHAT WOULD MAKE THIS GUARD FAIL, so a future reader knows what it is watching ==
   FAIL "red fixture passed unexpectedly" if the declared bin ever gets hit, which means either the injecting
   RAM is announced wrongly or a data injection ran without its knob. FAIL on a red_expect mismatch if the
   checker's reason wording changes, which is a signature drift to fix in the entry rather than a defect.
   NOT_RUN if no stage checks the expectation, which is the parked state above and needs no action.
   And if the manifest is ever dropped from the entry the loader still ACCEPTS it, silently turning the guard
   into a sim-log-graded fixture whose simulation passes by construction, which then reports the fixture
   passing unexpectedly on every run. The manifest is part of the entry, not an optional extra.
# END. Every figure above is read from the run's own artefacts by the command printed above it. The run
# directories are scratchpad paths and not durable, which is why this log is the record rather than a pointer.
